QUESTIONS.push(
{id:'qpc-cq4-1', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":2,"module":6,"lecture":"qpc-L08","topic":"qpc-multi","sub":"qpc-superpos","concept":"why-multiple-dosing","skill":"recall","source":"both","stem":"Why are multiple dosage regimens are used?","options":[{"t":"to maintain plasma drug levels within the therapeutic window","correct":true,"why":"One dose rises above the minimum effective concentration and then falls below it. Repeating the dose keeps the level between the minimum effective and the minimum toxic concentration, the therapeutic window."},{"t":"to decrease the MEC","correct":false,"why":"Picking this treats the MEC as something a regimen can move. The minimum effective concentration (MEC) is a property of the drug and the response it produces, fixed for a given patient. Dosing does not lower it; dosing raises the plasma level so that it reaches the MEC and stays above it between doses."},{"t":"to decrease the rate of elimination of drug from the body","correct":false,"why":"Picking this reads repeated dosing as a brake on elimination. Elimination is set by clearance, a property of the patient and the drug, which the schedule cannot change. A repeated dose replaces the drug that elimination has removed since the last dose; the fraction removed each hour is the same as after a single dose."},{"t":"to increase the clearance of drugs","correct":false,"why":"Picking this takes a parameter of the patient for an aim of the regimen. Clearance belongs to the patient and the drug, not to the schedule, so no regimen can raise it; and a higher clearance would lower the plasma levels, the opposite of what the regimen is for."}],"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["Her drawing on the single-dose curve adds two lines, the minimum toxic concentration (MTC) above and the minimum effective concentration (MEC) below: \"our goal is that we want to stay in between these two lines.\"","A single dose stays between them only for a while, because first-order elimination carries the level back below the MEC. Multiple doses, or an infusion, replace what is eliminated and hold the level there.","Dose and interval are then chosen so that the steady-state peak stays below the MTC and the trough above the MEC (6a slide \"Consider Peak and Trough\").","The textbook states the same purpose: multiple-dosage or infusion regimens keep plasma levels within the therapeutic window."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 4 (Modules 6–7a), question 1; transcript, the MTC and MEC lines drawn on the single-dose curve (TRANSCRIPT_CUES.md); Shargel 7e Chapter 9, printout p. 1"},
{id:'qpc-cq4-2', type:'numeric', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":2,"module":6,"lecture":"qpc-L08","topic":"qpc-multi","sub":"qpc-ssbolus","concept":"cmax-ss","skill":"multidose","stem":"What is the expected maximum steady-state concentration following IV bolus injections of 17 mg/kg to a 75 kg male every 8 hours? The drug has an elimination half-life of 4 hours, and the apparent volume of distribution is 15% of body weight. Give your answer in (mg/L), rounded to the nearest tenth (one decimal place) with no units.","units":"mg/L","answer":151.1,"tol":7.6,"steps":[{"k":"setup","t":"Cmax∞ = {{frac:C0|1 − e^(−kτ)}}, with C0 = {{frac:D0|VD}}","why":"\"IV bolus injections … every 8 hours\" and \"maximum steady-state concentration\" name the repeated IV bolus block at steady state. The peak at the plateau is the single-dose peak divided by the fraction not yet eliminated at each dose."},{"k":"unit","t":"D0 = 17 mg/kg × 75 kg = 1275 mg; VD = 0.15 × 75 kg = 11.25 L; C0 = {{frac:1275 mg|11.25 L}} = 113.3 mg/L","why":"Both the dose and the volume are given per kilogram of body weight, so both are scaled to the 75 kg patient before anything else. mg over L is mg/L."},{"k":"algebra","t":"k = {{frac:0.693|4 hr}} = 0.1733 hr⁻¹; e^(−kτ) = e^(−0.1733 × 8) = e^(−1.386) = 0.250","why":"Eight hours is two half-lives, so one quarter of a dose remains when the next arrives. The exponent is dimensionless: reciprocal hours times hours."},{"k":"algebra","t":"Cmax∞ = {{frac:113.3 mg/L|1 − 0.250}} = {{frac:113.3|0.750}} = 151.1 mg/L","why":"Dividing by 0.75 is the accumulation factor 1.33: the steady-state peak is one third higher than the first-dose peak."},{"k":"round","t":"151.1","why":"Her key is 151.1 with a margin of 5%, so 143.5 to 158.7 scores. One decimal place was asked for, and Canvas took the number with no unit."}],"givens":[["D0","17 mg/kg","scaled to 75 kg: 1275 mg, the numerator of C0"],["weight","75 kg","scales both the dose and the volume"],["τ","every 8 hours","the exponent kτ = 0.1733 × 8 = 1.386"],["t½","4 hours","gives k = {{frac:0.693|4}} = 0.1733 hr⁻¹"],["VD","15% of body weight","scaled to 75 kg: 11.25 L, the denominator of C0"]],"check":{"t":"C0 is {{frac:17|0.15}} = 113.3 mg/L per dose and 8 hours is two half-lives, so a quarter is left at each new dose; the peak is C0 over 0.750, a third more than 113.3: 151.1, between 113.3 and 2 × 113.3.","lo":113.3,"hi":226.6},"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["Scale per-kilogram values to the patient first; the mg/kg and L/kg cancel against the same body weight.","τ equal to two half-lives leaves 25% at each dose, so the factor is 1 ÷ 0.75.","The same line, with e^(−kτ) multiplied in on top, gives the trough."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 4 (Modules 6–7a), question 2; 6---Repetitive-IV-Bolus-and-Intermittent-IV-Infusions.pdf, slide \"Concentration of Drug in the Body at Steady-State Following Repeated IV Bolus Injections\""},
{id:'qpc-cq4-3', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":2,"module":6,"lecture":"qpc-L09","topic":"qpc-multoral","sub":"qpc-oparam","concept":"raise-css-by-regimen","skill":"apply","dupOf":"qpc-m6b-c16","source":"both","stem":"Which is the best option to increase the steady-state concentration in a dosing regimen?","options":[{"t":"increase the dosing interval","correct":false,"why":"A longer interval gives elimination more time between doses, so the steady-state level falls and the swing between peak and trough grows. This is the direction many pick; it is the opposite of what is asked."},{"t":"decrease the dosing interval","correct":true,"why":"Dosing more often puts the next dose in before as much of the last has left, so the plateau rises. Of the two things a regimen can change, the dose and the interval, this is the interval change that raises the level."},{"t":"increase the clearance","correct":false,"why":"Picking this treats clearance as a dosing choice. Clearance (Cl) belongs to the patient; a regimen can change only the dose and the interval. A higher clearance would lower the level: Cavg∞ (the average steady-state concentration) = {{frac:F D0|Cl × τ}}, with F the bioavailable fraction, D0 the dose and τ the interval."},{"t":"decrease the elimination half-life","correct":false,"why":"Picking this treats the half-life as something the prescriber sets. The half-life is a property of the drug in the patient, {{frac:0.693|k}} with k the elimination rate constant, not a dosing choice. A shorter half-life would also mean faster loss between doses and a lower steady-state level, the opposite of what is asked."}],"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["Her rule: the only things a regimen can change are the size of the dose and the dosing interval; clearance and half-life are not choices.","Slide \"Altering Dosing Interval\": decreasing the interval increases steady-state concentrations and decreases the peak-to-trough fluctuation, at the cost of compliance.","Cmax∞, Cmin∞ and Cavg∞ all go up; the trough sits closer to the peak because the next dose arrives before the level has fallen as far.","Cavg∞ = {{frac:F D0|Cl × τ}} has τ in the denominator, and a shorter interval leaves more of each dose when the next arrives, so the accumulation factor {{frac:1|1 − e^(−kτ)}} rises."]},{"h":"Her Example 1 three ways","fig":"dose_interval","list":["Every 8 hours, every 4 hours, and double the dose every 8 hours: the shorter interval raises all three lines and pulls the trough toward the peak."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 4 (Modules 6–7a), question 3; 6a---Multiple-Oral-Doses.pdf, slides \"Altering Steady-State Concentrations\" and \"Altering Dosing Interval\"; 09.28 lecture"},
{id:'qpc-m6b-c16', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":2,"module":6,"lecture":"qpc-L09","topic":"qpc-multoral","sub":"qpc-oparam","concept":"decrease-interval-fluctuation","skill":"apply","source":"both","stem":"The dosing interval of an oral regimen is shortened from 8 hours to 4 hours, same dose. Why does the fluctuation between peak and trough decrease?","options":[{"t":"Less time passes for the level to fall","correct":true,"why":"The trough is where the next dose interrupts the decline. With 4 hours instead of 8, the decline stops earlier, so the trough sits closer to the peak, a larger fraction of it, even though both peak and trough are higher."},{"t":"Less drug accumulates, so the peaks are lower","why":"This has accumulation backwards. A shorter interval leaves more of each dose behind when the next arrives, so more accumulates and the peaks are higher; the swing shrinks because the troughs rise by a larger fraction than the peaks."},{"t":"The elimination rate constant falls","why":"Choosing this changes k. The regimen changes only how often the drug is given; k, the half-life and clearance are unchanged."},{"t":"Absorption becomes slower","why":"Choosing this changes ka. Each dose is absorbed with the same ka whatever the interval; the smaller swing comes from the shorter decline between doses."}],"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["Her reasoning: 3 to 5 half-lives clear most of a dose.","A shorter interval cuts into that time, so the level does not get near the bottom of the curve before the next dose arrives.","So Cmax∞ and Cmin∞ both go up; what decreases is how far the level falls between them.","In her Example 1 the trough is a quarter of the peak every 8 hours and half of it every 4 hours."]},{"h":"Her Example 1 three ways","fig":"dose_interval","after":"Her Example 1 (each dose adds 40 mg/L, t½ 4 hours) every 8 hours, every 4 hours, and at double the dose."}],"cite":"6a---Multiple-Oral-Doses.pdf, slide \"Altering Dosing Interval, second slide\"; 09.28 lecture","quote":"if we decrease that, um, dosing interval, then maybe we're cutting into that 3 to 5 half-lives a little bit more, so we're not going all the way to the bottom of that curve"},
{id:'qpc-cq4-4', type:'numeric', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":2,"module":6,"lecture":"qpc-L08","topic":"qpc-intermit","sub":"qpc-add","concept":"sum-two-infusions","skill":"multidose","stem":"Four hundred milligrams of an antibiotic was infused intravenously over a period of 2 hours. Eight hours after the start of the first infusion, a second 400-mg dose was infused again over a period of 2 hours. The half-life of the antibiotic is approximately 4 hours and the apparent volume of distribution is approximately 20 L. What is the plasma drug concentration 4 hours after the cessation of the second infusion? Give your answer in mg/L rounded to the nearest tenth (one decimal place) with no units.","units":"mg/L","answer":10.6,"tol":0.53,"steps":[{"k":"setup","t":"Each infusion ends at Cend = {{frac:R|k × VD}}(1 − e^(−k × 2 hr)); then Cp = Cend e^(−k t₁) + Cend e^(−k t₂), one term per infusion","why":"The two infusions are added (superposition). Each is a 2-hour infusion that ends at the same concentration because the dose, the time and the patient are the same; after it stops, each decays by first-order elimination from its own end time."},{"k":"unit","t":"R = {{frac:400 mg|2 hr}} = 200 mg/hr; k = {{frac:0.693|4 hr}} = 0.1733 hr⁻¹; Cl = k × VD = 0.1733 × 20 L = 3.465 L/hr","why":"The rate is the dose over the infusion time, in mg/hr. Clearance comes from k and VD because the infusion line wants Cl."},{"k":"algebra","t":"Cend = {{frac:200 mg/hr|3.465 L/hr}}(1 − e^(−0.1733 × 2)) = 57.72 × (1 − 0.7071) = 16.90 mg/L","why":"57.72 mg/L would be the plateau if the drip ran on; after two hours, half a half-life, it has reached 29.3% of that."},{"k":"algebra","t":"Times since each infusion ended: first ended at 2 hr, so 12 hr have passed at t = 14 hr; second ended at 10 hr, so 4 hr. Cp = 16.90 e^(−0.1733 × 12) + 16.90 e^(−0.1733 × 4) = 16.90 × 0.125 + 16.90 × 0.500 = 2.11 + 8.45 = 10.56 mg/L","why":"The second infusion started 8 hours after the first began, so it ran from 8 to 10 hours; 4 hours after its end is 14 hours. Twelve hours is three half-lives (one eighth left), four hours is one half-life (one half left)."},{"k":"round","t":"10.6","why":"Her key is 10.6 with a margin of 5%, so 10.1 to 11.1 scores. Leaving out the first infusion gives 8.45, too low; treating each infusion as a 400 mg bolus into 20 L gives 20 × 0.125 + 20 × 0.500 = 12.5, too high, because each 2-hour infusion ends at 16.90 mg/L."}],"givens":[["D0","Four hundred milligrams","with the infusion time gives R = {{frac:400|2}} = 200 mg/hr"],["infusion time","over a period of 2 hours","R = 200 mg/hr; t = 2 hr in 1 − e^(−kt)"],["second start","Eight hours after the start of the first infusion","the second infusion runs 8 to 10 hr"],["t½","approximately 4 hours","gives k = {{frac:0.693|4}} = 0.1733 hr⁻¹"],["VD","approximately 20 L","in Cl = kVD = 3.465 L/hr"],["t","4 hours after the cessation of the second infusion","the second term decays 4 hr, the first 12 hr"]],"check":{"t":"Each infusion ends at 16.90 mg/L. Four hours is one half-life, so the second leaves 8.45; the first, 12 hours or three half-lives on, leaves an eighth, 2.11. The sum is between 8.45 and 2 × 16.90.","lo":8.45,"hi":33.8},"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["Work one infusion to its end level with the infusion line, then let each copy decay from its own stopping time, then add.","Count the clock from the start of the first infusion: starts at 0 and 8 hours, ends at 2 and 10 hours, the question asks about 14 hours.","Half-life arithmetic checks the exponentials: 12 hours is three half-lives (0.125), 4 hours is one (0.5)."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 4 (Modules 6–7a), question 4; 6---Repetitive-IV-Bolus-and-Intermittent-IV-Infusions.pdf, slides \"Administering One or More Doses by IV Infusion\" and \"Example 4\""},
{id:'qpc-cq4-5', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":2,"module":6,"lecture":"qpc-L09","topic":"qpc-multoral","sub":"qpc-oparam","concept":"increase-dose-effects","skill":"apply","dupOf":"qpc-m6b-c14","source":"both","stem":"Which of the following results if the dose is increased on a medication that is to be administered orally at regularly repeating intervals?","options":[{"t":"Increased steady-state tmax","correct":false,"why":"Picking this reads a larger dose as a slower climb to the peak. tmax∞ (the time of the peak within an interval at steady state) depends on ka (the absorption rate constant), k (the elimination rate constant) and τ (the dosing interval) only. The dose scales the curve, not the peak time."},{"t":"Decreased rate of absorption","correct":false,"why":"Picking this treats the absorption rate constant as adjustable by the dose. ka (the absorption rate constant) is a property of the drug and the dosage form. A larger dose means more drug absorbed per hour at the same ka, since the rate is ka × the amount at the absorption site."},{"t":"Increased steady-state Cmax","correct":true,"why":"Every concentration in the regimen scales with the dose, so the steady-state peak rises in proportion. Slide \"Altering Dose\": increasing the dose increases steady-state concentrations and the peak-to-trough fluctuation."},{"t":"Decreased clearance","correct":false,"why":"Picking this takes the amount cleared per hour for the clearance. Clearance (Cl) is the volume of plasma cleared of drug per hour, a property of the patient and the drug. A larger dose raises the concentration and so the amount removed per hour, Cl × concentration, but Cl itself does not change."}],"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["Dose up: higher Cmax, Cmin and Cavg, a bigger swing between peak and trough, usually no change in compliance.","Dose does not move tmax, ka or clearance.","The swing grows because Dmax∞ − Dmin∞ equals the dose, so the gap between peak and trough scales with the dose. The trough stays the same fraction of the peak."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 4 (Modules 6–7a), question 5; 6a---Multiple-Oral-Doses.pdf, slides \"Poll EV\" (page 1) and \"Altering Dose\"; 09.28 lecture"},
{id:'qpc-m6b-c14', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":2,"module":6,"lecture":"qpc-L09","topic":"qpc-multoral","sub":"qpc-oparam","concept":"increase-dose-effects","skill":"apply","source":"both","stem":"Which of the following would be expected at steady state if the oral dose of a drug is doubled with no change to the dosing interval?","options":[{"t":"Higher concentrations and a larger swing between peak and trough","correct":true,"why":"A larger dose raises every concentration in proportion, so the peak, trough and average all rise, and the peak-to-trough gap, a fixed fraction of the peak, grows with them. Compliance usually does not change, because the patient takes the same number of doses."},{"t":"Higher concentrations and a smaller swing between peak and trough","why":"This pairs the right direction for the level with the wrong one for the swing. Doubling the dose doubles the peak and the trough, so the difference between them doubles too."},{"t":"The same average concentration, reached sooner","why":"This confuses the dose with the half-life. The dose sets how high the plateau is, not how fast it is reached; steady state still takes 3 to 5 half-lives."},{"t":"A longer time to peak","why":"Choosing this attaches the dose to tmax. Neither the single-dose tmax nor tmax∞ contains the dose, so doubling it leaves the timing alone."}],"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["Method 1 for altering steady state: change the dose, keep the interval.","Fluctuation, or swing, is how far the level falls from the peak to the trough within one dosing interval.","The whole curve scales up: peak, trough and average all double when the dose doubles, and so does the gap; in her Example 1 it doubles from 40 to 80 mg/L.","The trough stays the same fraction of the peak, because that fraction, e^(−kτ), depends on the interval and k, not on the dose.","Her slide \"Altering Dose\" calls this \"increased fluctuation\"."]},{"h":"Her Example 1 three ways","fig":"dose_interval","after":"Her Example 1 (each dose adds 40 mg/L, t½ 4 hours) every 8 hours, every 4 hours, and at double the dose."}],"cite":"6a---Multiple-Oral-Doses.pdf, slides \"Altering Dose\" and \"Altering Dose, second slide\"; 09.28 lecture","quote":"if we increase the dose, we expect increased concentration and increased fluctuation between peaks and troughs"},
{id:'qpc-cq4-6', type:'numeric', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":2,"module":6,"lecture":"qpc-L09","topic":"qpc-multoral","sub":"qpc-ossc","concept":"tmax-ss","skill":"multidose","stem":"A 74-kg patient was administered a 750 mg oral dose of a new drug every 8 hours. The drug has an oral bioavailability of 92%, apparent volume of distribution of 0.29 L/kg, elimination half-life of 5 hours, and the absorption half-life of 1.5 hour. When does the expected maximum concentration of drug in the plasma at steady-state occur? Give your answer in hours rounded to the nearest tenth (one decimal place) with no units.","units":"hr","answer":2.6,"tol":0.13,"steps":[{"k":"setup","t":"tmax∞ = {{frac:1|ka − k}} ln[{{frac:ka(1 − e^(−kτ))|k(1 − e^(−kaτ))}}]","why":"\"Oral dose … every 8 hours\" and \"when does the … maximum concentration … at steady-state occur\" ask for the time of the peak within an interval at the plateau. Only the two rate constants and τ enter; the dose, F, VD and the weight are not needed."},{"k":"algebra","t":"k = {{frac:0.693|5 hr}} = 0.1386 hr⁻¹; ka = {{frac:0.693|1.5 hr}} = 0.462 hr⁻¹","why":"Each half-life gives its own rate constant. The absorption half-life is the shorter one, so ka is the larger constant, as it must be for an immediate-release product."},{"k":"algebra","t":"e^(−kτ) = e^(−0.1386 × 8) = 0.330, so 1 − 0.330 = 0.670; e^(−kaτ) = e^(−0.462 × 8) = 0.0248, so 1 − 0.0248 = 0.975","why":"Both accumulation terms are evaluated at τ = 8 hours. Absorption is nearly complete within an interval (0.975), elimination is not (0.670)."},{"k":"algebra","t":"tmax∞ = {{frac:1|0.462 − 0.1386}} ln[{{frac:0.462 × 0.670|0.1386 × 0.975}}] = {{frac:1|0.3234}} ln(2.291) = 3.092 × 0.829 = 2.56 hr","why":"The ratio inside the log is 2.291 and its natural log is 0.829; dividing by the difference of the constants gives hours. The single-dose tmax for the same constants would be 3.72 hr, so the steady-state peak comes earlier."},{"k":"round","t":"2.6","why":"Her key is 2.6 with a margin of 5%, so 2.5 to 2.7 scores. The single-dose line, {{frac:ln(ka ÷ k)|ka − k}}, gives 3.72 hr with the same constants; at steady state the peak comes earlier, so the τ terms are needed."}],"givens":[["weight","74-kg","not needed: tmax∞ has no volume in it"],["D0","750 mg","not needed: tmax∞ does not depend on the dose"],["τ","every 8 hours","in 1 − e^(−kτ) and 1 − e^(−kaτ)"],["F","92%","not needed: F scales the height of the curve, not its timing"],["VD","0.29 L/kg","not needed"],["t½","5 hours","gives k = {{frac:0.693|5}} = 0.1386 hr⁻¹"],["absorption t½","1.5 hour","gives ka = {{frac:0.693|1.5}} = 0.462 hr⁻¹"]],"check":{"t":"The steady-state peak must fall inside the 8-hour interval and comes earlier than a first-dose peak, since drug from earlier doses is being eliminated while the new dose absorbs: 2.6 hours, between 0 and 8.","lo":0,"hi":8},"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["The steady-state peak time needs ka, k and τ only; extra data in the stem (F, VD, weight, dose) is for other parts of the same problem.","At steady state the peak arrives earlier than after a single dose, because drug left from earlier doses is already being eliminated while the new dose is absorbed.","Each half-life in the stem is converted to its own rate constant with 0.693."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 4 (Modules 6–7a), question 6; 6a---Multiple-Oral-Doses.pdf, slide \"Example 1\" (tmax at steady state)"},
{id:'qpc-cq4-7', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":2,"module":6,"lecture":"qpc-L08","topic":"qpc-intermit","sub":"qpc-why","concept":"intermittent-assumptions","skill":"recall","source":"both","stem":"Which of the following best describes the assumptions associated with intermittent IV infusions?","options":[{"t":"all drugs are better tolerated as IV bolus doses compared to IV infusion","correct":false,"why":"The rationale slide says the reverse: many drugs are better tolerated when infused slowly over time than as an IV bolus. The word \"all\" also overstates it."},{"t":"intermittent IV infusions decrease elimination half-life which leads to increased efficacy","correct":false,"why":"Picking this treats a route of administration as a change to a drug property. The elimination half-life is {{frac:0.693|k}}, with k (the elimination rate constant) set by clearance and volume of distribution, both properties of the drug in the patient. Infusing rather than injecting changes how fast drug enters, not how fast it leaves."},{"t":"concentrations of drug in the body following multiple infusions are additive","correct":true,"why":"This is superposition applied to infusions: each infusion is worked as a single infusion, decays from its own end, and the concentrations are added. It is the assumption behind every two-infusion calculation."},{"t":"intermittent IV infusion is the best way to very rapidly achieve high drug concentrations","correct":false,"why":"Picking this reverses the rationale for infusing. Spreading a dose over time is done to prevent high concentrations and their side effects; the concentration at the end of an infusion is lower than the peak the same dose would give as a bolus. A loading bolus, not an infusion, reaches a high level quickly."}],"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["Rationale slide: prevent high drug concentrations and accompanying side effects; many drugs are better tolerated when infused slowly over time than by IV bolus.","Assumption: concentrations from successive infusions add (superposition), and each infusion follows the single-infusion equation with the same R, k and VD.","They add because elimination is first order: drug from each infusion is removed at the same fraction per hour whether or not the other is present (slide \"Superposition\")."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 4 (Modules 6–7a), question 7; 6---Repetitive-IV-Bolus-and-Intermittent-IV-Infusions.pdf, slides \"Rationale\" and \"Administering One or More Doses by IV Infusion\"; 09.28 lecture"},
{id:'qpc-cq4-8', type:'numeric', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":2,"module":6,"lecture":"qpc-L09","topic":"qpc-multoral","sub":"qpc-ossc","concept":"dose-for-cavg","skill":"multidose","stem":"A patient is to take a medication by mouth every 8 hours. The medication has an oral bioavailability of 83%, an absorption half-life of 90 minutes, total body clearance of 3.0 L/hr, and apparent volume of distribution of approximately 25 L. What is an appropriate dose to achieve an average plasma drug concentration of 25 mg/L in this patient? Give your answer in mg rounded to the nearest whole number with no units.","units":"mg","answer":723,"tol":36,"steps":[{"k":"setup","t":"Cavg∞ = {{frac:F × D0|Cl × τ}}, so D0 = {{frac:Cavg∞ × Cl × τ|F}}","why":"\"By mouth every 8 hours\" and \"average plasma drug concentration\" name the average steady-state line of the multiple oral block, rearranged for the dose. Clearance is given directly, so k and VD are not needed; the absorption half-life and VD are extra data for this part."},{"k":"algebra","t":"D0 = {{frac:25 mg/L × 3.0 L/hr × 8 hr|0.83}} = {{frac:600 mg|0.83}} = 722.9 mg","why":"mg/L × L/hr × hr leaves mg: the amount cleared in one interval at the target average. Dividing by F scales it up to the dose that has to be swallowed for 83% of it to reach the blood."},{"k":"round","t":"723","why":"Her key is 723 with a margin of 5%, so 687 to 759 scores. Multiplying by F instead of dividing gives 600 × 0.83 = 498 mg, less than the 600 mg that has to be absorbed, which cannot be right. The whole number was asked for."}],"givens":[["τ","every 8 hours","multiplies clearance: Cl × τ = 3.0 × 8 = 24 L"],["F","83%","written 0.83, the denominator"],["absorption t½","90 minutes","not needed: the average concentration does not depend on ka"],["Cl","3.0 L/hr","in the numerator with τ"],["VD","approximately 25 L","not needed: clearance is given"],["Cavg∞","25 mg/L","the target, in the numerator"]],"check":{"t":"Holding 25 mg/L while 24 L is cleared per interval needs 600 mg absorbed; with only 0.83 of the dose absorbed, the dose must be larger than 600 mg: {{frac:600|0.83}} = 723 mg.","lo":600},"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["The average steady-state concentration depends on the dosing rate F × D0 ÷ τ and on clearance, and on nothing else: not on ka, not on VD.","Cl × τ × Cavg∞ is the amount of drug cleared per interval; the dose has to replace it, scaled up by F.","She rounds a dose to a usable strength on worksheets; here Canvas asked for the whole number."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 4 (Modules 6–7a), question 8; 6a---Multiple-Oral-Doses.pdf, slide \"Peak, Trough and Average Plasma Concentrations at Steady State\""},
{id:'qpc-cq4-9', type:'numeric', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":2,"module":7,"lecture":"qpc-L10","topic":"qpc-bioavail","sub":"qpc-fabs","concept":"fabs-from-clearance","skill":"bioavail","stem":"What is the absolute bioavailability of a drug for which a 500 mg dose resulted in an AUC of 139 (mg/L)hr? The elimination half-life of the drug is 4.5 hr and the apparent volume of distribution is 18 L. Give your answer as a decimal rounded to the nearest hundredth with no units.","units":"(as a decimal fraction)","answer":0.77,"tol":0.023,"steps":[{"k":"setup","t":"Fabs = {{frac:AUCpo|AUCIV}} with the same dose, and AUCIV = {{frac:DIV|Cl}}, Cl = k × VD","why":"\"Absolute bioavailability\" with one AUC given and no IV AUC: the IV area has to come from the dose and the clearance, which the half-life and the volume supply. With equal doses the dose ratio is 1."},{"k":"algebra","t":"k = {{frac:0.693|4.5 hr}} = 0.154 hr⁻¹; Cl = 0.154 hr⁻¹ × 18 L = 2.772 L/hr; AUCIV = {{frac:500 mg|2.772 L/hr}} = 180.4 (mg/L)hr","why":"hr⁻¹ × L is L/hr, and mg over L/hr is mg·hr/L, the unit of an AUC. This is the area the same 500 mg would give intravenously."},{"k":"algebra","t":"Fabs = {{frac:139|180.4}} = 0.771","why":"With equal doses the dose ratio is {{frac:500|500}} = 1 and drops out, leaving Fabs = {{frac:AUCpo|AUCIV}} = {{frac:139|180.4}} = 0.771, a dimensionless fraction. The oral area is 77 per cent of the IV area, so 77 per cent of the oral dose reached the circulation."},{"k":"round","t":"0.77","why":"Her key is 0.77 with a margin of 3%, so 0.75 to 0.79 scores. Two decimal places were asked for, as a decimal, not a percentage."}],"givens":[["D0","500 mg","the numerator of AUCIV = {{frac:DIV|Cl}}; the dose ratio is 1"],["AUCpo","139 (mg/L)hr","the numerator of the area ratio"],["t½","4.5 hr","gives k = {{frac:0.693|4.5}} = 0.154 hr⁻¹"],["VD","18 L","in Cl = kVD = 0.154 × 18 = 2.772 L/hr"]],"check":{"t":"Clearance 2.772 L/hr means the 500 mg would give 180.4 (mg/L)hr if all of it reached the blood; the oral 139 is about three-quarters of that, so F is about 0.77, between 0 and 1.","lo":0,"hi":1},"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["When the IV curve is not given, dose over clearance is the IV AUC.","Equal oral and IV doses make the dose ratio 1, so F is the ratio of the areas.","Report F as a decimal with two places (0.77), or as 77%; never 0.771 when two places are asked."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 4 (Modules 6–7a), question 9; 7a---Bioavailability-and-Bioequivalence.pdf, slide \"Absolute Bioavailability\" (equation) and the in-class activity of 09.30, question 3"},
{id:'qpc-cq4-10', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":2,"module":7,"lecture":"qpc-L10","topic":"qpc-bioavail","sub":"qpc-defs","concept":"absolute-vs-relative","skill":"recall","dupOf":"qpc-m7-c03","source":"slide","stem":"Which term describes the comparison of the bioavailability of the active drug in the systemic circulation following extravascular administration with the bioavailability of the same drug following intravenous administration?","options":[{"t":"Absolute Bioavailability","correct":true,"why":"The stem is the slide definition word for word. Absolute means measured against the intravenous dose, where all of the drug is in the circulation."},{"t":"Bioequivalence","correct":false,"why":"Picking this takes a two-product comparison for the comparison against IV. Bioequivalence compares a test product with a reference product of the same drug in both rate and extent, and neither product is the IV dose. The stem names intravenous administration as the comparator, which is the mark of absolute bioavailability."},{"t":"Relative Bioavailability","correct":false,"why":"Picking this reads the IV comparison as a comparison between two ordinary products. Relative bioavailability compares two formulations or routes against a reference that is not the IV dose, such as a tablet against an oral solution. The stem sets the extravascular dose against the same drug given intravenously, which makes the comparison absolute."},{"t":"Drug Product Performance","correct":false,"why":"Picking this takes the broad opening term for the specific comparison. Drug product performance is the release of the drug substance from the drug product leading to bioavailability, and it describes one product on its own. The stem describes a ratio between an extravascular route and the IV route, which is absolute bioavailability."}],"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["Absolute: against IV. Relative: against another product or route. Bioequivalence: two products of the same drug compared in both rate and extent.","The IV dose is the reference because the whole IV dose enters the circulation, so its F is 1; Fabs = {{frac:AUCpo|AUCIV}} × {{frac:DIV|Dpo}} cannot come out above 1.","She quotes the slide definition and asks for the term; the words \"intravenous administration\" settle this one."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 4 (Modules 6–7a), question 10; 7a---Bioavailability-and-Bioequivalence.pdf, slide \"Absolute Bioavailability\" (page 5)"},
{id:'qpc-m7-c03', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":2,"module":7,"lecture":"qpc-L10","topic":"qpc-bioavail","sub":"qpc-defs","concept":"absolute-vs-relative","skill":"tell","dupOf":"qpc-cq4-10","source":"both","stem":"What does absolute bioavailability compare?","options":[{"t":"An extravascular dose against an IV dose of the same drug","correct":true,"why":"Absolute means compared with the IV (intravenous) dose, where F is 1 because the whole dose is in the circulation. The oral (extravascular) AUC is set against the IV AUC, with the doses corrected for if they differ."},{"t":"Two different formulations of the same drug","why":"Picking this takes relative bioavailability for absolute. Comparing two formulations of the same drug, neither of them IV (intravenous), is relative bioavailability, with one of the two chosen as the reference. Absolute bioavailability always has the IV dose as the reference, because only an IV dose puts the whole dose in the circulation."},{"t":"A test product with a reference product at the same molar dose","why":"Picking this takes a bioequivalence study for an absolute bioavailability measurement. A test product against a reference product at the same molar dose is the bioequivalence comparison, which asks about rate and extent together. Absolute bioavailability is a single ratio of AUCs (areas under the curve) with the IV AUC in the denominator."},{"t":"The dose given with the amount excreted unchanged in the urine","why":"This reads the fraction excreted unchanged, fe, as bioavailability. fe splits elimination between the kidney and the liver; bioavailability asks how much of a dose reached the circulation at all."}],"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["Absolute: compared with the IV dose.","Relative: compared with another formulation of the same drug.","In both, the thing compared with sits in the denominator."]},{"h":"Absolute, relative and bioequivalent, side by side","table":{"head":["","Absolute","Relative","Bioequivalence"],"rows":[["Compared with","the IV dose of the same drug","another formulation of the same drug","a reference product"],["Measures","extent","extent","rate and extent"],["Above 1?","No","Yes","A judgement, not a number"]]},"after":"A bioequivalence study is a specialized type of relative bioavailability study: it adds the rate to the extent."}],"cite":"7a---Bioavailability-and-Bioequivalence.pdf, slide \"Absolute Bioavailability\"; transcript 09-30","quote":"So, absolute says we are comparing basically to the IV."},
{id:'qpc-cq5-1', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":3,"module":9,"lecture":"qpc-L11","topic":"qpc-pkpd","sub":"qpc-thalf","concept":"thalf-double-teff","skill":"apply","dupOf":"qpc-pd-c20","source":"slide","stem":"For a drug that displays linear, first-order pharmacokinetics, what is the impact on duration of activity if there is an increase in elimination half-life?","options":[{"t":"There is a no change to the duration of activity.","correct":false,"why":"Picking this treats the duration as set by the dose alone. In teff = 1.44 t½ ln({{frac:D0|VD × Ceff}}) the half-life multiplies the whole expression, so a longer t½ with the same dose and volume keeps the level above the minimum effective concentration (MEC) for longer."},{"t":"Duration of activity will increase.","correct":true,"why":"A longer half-life means slower elimination, so the level takes longer to fall from C0 to the MEC. teff is in direct proportion to t½: twice the half-life, twice the duration, as when decreased renal or hepatic function prolongs t½."},{"t":"Duration of activity will decrease.","correct":false,"why":"Picking this reverses the direction. A longer t½ means a smaller elimination rate constant k, so the level falls more slowly and stays at or above the MEC for longer, not shorter."}],"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["teff = 1.44 t½ ln({{frac:D0|VD × Ceff}}): t½ multiplies everything, so teff changes in direct proportion to the half-life.","The dose sits inside the logarithm: doubling the dose adds one half-life to teff; it does not double it.","Decreased renal or hepatic elimination prolongs t½, prolongs the effect, and raises the possibility of toxicity."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 5 (Modules 7b and 8), question 1; 7b---Pharmacokinetics-and-Pharmacodynamics.pdf, slides 9 \"Effect of Dose and Elimination Half-life on Duration of Activity\" and 10 \"Effect of Elimination Half-life on Duration of Activity\""},
{id:'qpc-pd-c20', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":3,"module":9,"lecture":"qpc-L11","topic":"qpc-pkpd","sub":"qpc-thalf","concept":"thalf-double-teff","skill":"apply","source":"slide","stem":"What is the expected change in the duration of activity of a single IV bolus dose if decreased elimination doubles the elimination half-life, with the dose and the volume of distribution unchanged?","options":[{"t":"teff doubles","correct":true,"why":"In teff = 1.44 t½ ln({{frac:D0|VD × Ceff}}), t½ multiplies the whole expression and nothing else changes, so teff is directly proportional to t½: twice the half-life, twice the duration."},{"t":"teff increases by one half-life","why":"Choosing this carries the dose rule over to the half-life. Doubling the dose adds one t½ because the dose is inside the logarithm; t½ multiplies the whole expression, so doubling it doubles teff."},{"t":"teff is halved","why":"Choosing this reverses the direction. A longer half-life means slower elimination, so the level takes longer to fall to Ceff and the drug acts longer, not shorter."},{"t":"teff does not change, because the dose is the same","why":"Choosing this ties duration to the dose alone. Onset, intensity and duration depend on the dose and on the pharmacokinetics; from the same C0, a slower decline keeps the level above Ceff longer."}],"teach":[{"h":"Elimination half-life and duration","list":["Alteration of the elimination processes, excretion and metabolism, changes the t½ of the drug.","In certain disease states, pathophysiologic changes in hepatic or renal function decrease elimination, seen as a prolonged t½.","The prolonged t½ retains drug in the body, increasing the duration of activity and the possibility of toxicity.","teff = 1.44 t½ ln({{frac:D0|VD × Ceff}}): teff is directly proportional to t½, but not to the dose."]}],"cite":"7b---Pharmacokinetics-and-Pharmacodynamics.pdf, slide 9 \"Effect of Dose and Elimination Half-life on Duration of Activity\""},
{id:'qpc-cq5-2', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":3,"module":9,"lecture":"qpc-L11","topic":"qpc-pkpd","sub":"qpc-teff","concept":"dose-double-teff","skill":"apply","dupOf":"qpc-pd-c14","source":"slide","stem":"Which of the following results when the IV bolus dose of a drug displaying linear, first-order pharmacokinetics is doubled?","options":[{"t":"The half-life is decreased by half","correct":false,"why":"Picking this treats the half-life as dose-dependent. In linear, first-order pharmacokinetics t½ = {{frac:0.693|k}} and k does not change with the dose; a half-life that changes with dose is the mark of a saturable, nonlinear drug."},{"t":"The duration of action is doubled","correct":false,"why":"Picking this treats the duration as proportional to the dose. The dose is inside a logarithm, teff = {{frac:ln C0 − ln Ceff|k}}, so doubling it adds ln 2 over k, one half-life, rather than doubling teff."},{"t":"The half-life is doubled","correct":false,"why":"Picking this moves the half-life with the dose. A larger dose raises every concentration, but first-order elimination removes the same fraction per hour, so the half-life stays the same; the duration is what changes."},{"t":"The duration of action is increased by one half-life","correct":true,"why":"Doubling D0 doubles C0, and the level needs exactly one half-life to fall from 2C0 back to C0, after which it follows the original curve. teff rises by {{frac:0.693|k}}, one t½; each further doubling adds one more half-life."}],"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["teff = {{frac:ln C0 − ln Ceff|k}} with C0 = {{frac:D0|VD}}: 2D0 adds ln 2 to the top, so teff grows by {{frac:0.693|k}}, one half-life.","In linear, first-order kinetics the half-life does not move with the dose; the duration does.","For an antibiotic with VD 24 L and k 0.15 hr⁻¹, 250 mg lasts 26.35 hr and 500 mg lasts 30.97 hr: 4.62 hr more, one half-life."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 5 (Modules 7b and 8), question 2; 7b---Pharmacokinetics-and-Pharmacodynamics.pdf, slides 9 \"Effect of Dose and Elimination Half-life on Duration of Activity\" and 12 (PollEv)"},
{id:'qpc-pd-c14', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":3,"module":9,"lecture":"qpc-L11","dupOf":"qpc-pd-c13","topic":"qpc-pkpd","sub":"qpc-teff","concept":"dose-double-teff","skill":"apply","source":"slide","stem":"What is the expected change in the duration of activity (teff) when the single IV bolus dose of a drug that displays linear, first-order pharmacokinetics is doubled?","options":[{"t":"teff rises by one half-life","correct":true,"why":"teff = {{frac:ln C0 − ln Ceff|k}}. Doubling D0 doubles C0 and adds ln 2 to the top, so teff rises by {{frac:0.693|k}}, exactly one t½. Each further doubling of the dose adds one more half-life."},{"t":"teff doubles","why":"Choosing this treats teff as proportional to D0. D0 is inside the logarithm, so 2D0 adds {{frac:ln 2|k}} = {{frac:0.693|k}} = t½ to teff rather than doubling it."},{"t":"teff does not change","why":"Choosing this treats duration as a fixed property of the drug, like the half-life. The half-life does not change with dose, but teff does: a higher C0 takes longer to fall to Ceff."},{"t":"teff increases by two half-lives","why":"Choosing this takes a factor of four for a factor of two. Doubling adds one half-life; quadrupling, two doublings, adds two: for a drug with a 5-hr half-life, 500 to 2000 mg adds 10 hr, two half-lives."}],"teach":[{"h":"Duration of activity, teff","list":["teff: the time one IV bolus keeps the plasma level at or above Ceff, the minimum effective concentration.","From C = C0e^(−kt) with C = Ceff: teff = {{frac:ln C0 − ln Ceff|k}}, where C0 = {{frac:D0|VD}}, the level just after the bolus.","With k = {{frac:0.693|t½}}: teff = 1.44 t½ ln({{frac:D0|VD × Ceff}}); 1.44 is {{frac:1|0.693}}.","The dose is inside the logarithm, so doubling it adds {{frac:ln 2|k}}, one half-life, to teff; it does not double teff.","Built for a single IV bolus, a one-compartment open model and first-order elimination."]}],"cite":"7b---Pharmacokinetics-and-Pharmacodynamics.pdf, slide 9 \"Effect of Dose and Elimination Half-life on Duration of Activity\"; PK-PD-Practice-1---Solutions.pdf"},
{id:'qpc-pd-c13', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":3,"module":9,"lecture":"qpc-L11","topic":"qpc-pkpd","sub":"qpc-teff","concept":"dose-double-teff","skill":"apply","source":"slide","stem":"Doubling the IV bolus dose of a drug that follows linear, first-order pharmacokinetics results in a 2-fold increase in the duration of activity.","options":[{"t":"True","why":"Choosing this treats duration as proportional to dose. The dose sits inside a logarithm, teff = 1.44 t½ ln({{frac:D0|VD × Ceff}}), so doubling it adds ln 2 over k, one half-life, to teff instead of doubling it."},{"t":"False","correct":true,"why":"Doubling D0 doubles C0, and the level needs exactly one half-life to fall from 2C0 back to C0; after that it follows the old curve. teff grows by one t½: 26.35 hr becomes 30.97 hr when 250 mg is doubled to 500 mg (VD 24 L, k 0.15 hr⁻¹)."}],"teach":[{"h":"Duration of activity, teff","list":["teff: the time one IV bolus keeps the plasma level at or above Ceff, the minimum effective concentration.","From C = C0e^(−kt) with C = Ceff: teff = {{frac:ln C0 − ln Ceff|k}}, where C0 = {{frac:D0|VD}}, the level just after the bolus.","With k = {{frac:0.693|t½}}: teff = 1.44 t½ ln({{frac:D0|VD × Ceff}}); 1.44 is {{frac:1|0.693}}.","The dose is inside the logarithm, so doubling it adds {{frac:ln 2|k}}, one half-life, to teff; it does not double teff.","Built for a single IV bolus, a one-compartment open model and first-order elimination."]}],"cite":"7b---Pharmacokinetics-and-Pharmacodynamics.pdf, slide 12, PollEv (keyed False)"},
{id:'qpc-cq5-3', type:'numeric', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":3,"module":9,"lecture":"qpc-L11","topic":"qpc-pkpd","sub":"qpc-teff","concept":"teff-calc","skill":"pkpd","stem":"What is the duration of action following a 604 mg IV bolus dose of a drug that displays linear, first-order pharmacokinetics? The half-life of elimination is 5 hours, apparent volume of distribution is 18 L, and minimum effective concentration is 6 mcg/mL. Provide your answer in hours rounded to the nearest tenth (one decimal place) with no units.","units":"hr","answer":12.4,"tol":0.62,"steps":[{"k":"unit","t":"Ceff = 6 mcg/mL = 6 mg/L","why":"1 mcg/mL equals 1 mg/L, because multiplying top and bottom by 1000 turns mcg into mg and mL into L. With the dose in mg and the volume in L, C0 then comes out in the same unit as Ceff."},{"k":"setup","t":"teff = {{frac:ln C0 − ln Ceff|k}}, with C0 = {{frac:D0|VD}} and k = {{frac:0.693|t½}}","why":"\"Duration of action\" after one IV bolus, with t½, VD and the minimum effective concentration given: the natural-log duration line, the time the level takes to fall from C0 to Ceff."},{"k":"algebra","t":"k = {{frac:0.693|5 hr}} = 0.1386 hr⁻¹; C0 = {{frac:604 mg|18 L}} = 33.56 mg/L","why":"The half-life gives k, the fraction of the drug eliminated per hour, in hr⁻¹. C0 is the whole bolus spread through VD at time zero, the level the decline starts from; mg over L is mg/L, the unit of Ceff."},{"k":"algebra","t":"teff = {{frac:ln(33.56 ÷ 6)|0.1386 hr⁻¹}} = {{frac:ln 5.593|0.1386}} = {{frac:1.721|0.1386 hr⁻¹}} = 12.42 hr","why":"The level has to fall 5.593-fold before the effect ends; its natural log divided by k gives hours. The half-life form, 1.44 × 5 hr × 1.721 = 12.39 hr, gives the same tenth."},{"k":"round","t":"12.4","why":"Her key is 12.4 with a margin of 5%, so 11.8 to 13.0 scores. Using base-10 logs without the 2.3 gives {{frac:0.7476|0.1386}} = 5.39 hr, far too short; one decimal place was asked for, with no unit."}],"givens":[["D0","604 mg","the numerator of C0"],["t½","5 hours","gives k = {{frac:0.693|5}} = 0.1386 hr⁻¹"],["VD","18 L","divides the dose: C0 = 33.56 mg/L"],["Ceff","6 mcg/mL","equals 6 mg/L, the MEC"]],"check":{"t":"C0 ÷ Ceff = 5.593 is between 4 and 8, so teff is between two and three half-lives of 5 hr: 12.4 hr, between 10 and 15.","lo":10,"hi":15},"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["teff is the time C = C0e^(−kt) takes to fall from C0 = {{frac:D0|VD}} to Ceff, the minimum effective concentration.","Each halving of the ratio C0 ÷ Ceff is one half-life of duration, so the ratio between 4 and 8 puts teff between 2 and 3 half-lives.","mcg/mL and mg/L are the same concentration, so the MEC needs no conversion factor."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 5 (Modules 7b and 8), question 3; 7b---Pharmacokinetics-and-Pharmacodynamics.pdf, slides 6 \"Relationship Between Dose and Duration of Activity (teff)\" and 7 \"Practice Problem\""},
{id:'qpc-cq5-4', type:'numeric', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":3,"module":9,"lecture":"qpc-L11","topic":"qpc-pkpd","sub":"qpc-dosefor","concept":"dose-for-teff","skill":"pkpd","stem":"What dose of a medication, that is to be administered as a single IV bolus injections would provide a duration of action of 20 hours in a 75 kg patient? The drug displays linear, first-order pharmacokinetics, has an elimination half-life of 5.8 hours, apparent volume of distribution of 0.25 L/kg, and a minimum effective concentration of 4 mg/L. Provide your answer in mg rounded to the nearest whole number with no units.","units":"mg","answer":818,"tol":41,"steps":[{"k":"unit","t":"VD = 0.25 L/kg × 75 kg = 18.75 L; k = {{frac:0.693|5.8 hr}} = 0.1195 hr⁻¹","why":"The volume is given per kilogram, so it is scaled to the 75 kg patient first. The dose line has k in its exponent, so the half-life is turned into k."},{"k":"setup","t":"Ceff = {{frac:D0|VD}} e^(−k teff), so D0 = Ceff × VD × e^(k teff)","why":"C = C0e^(−kt) with C0 = D0 over VD, written at the moment the level reaches the MEC, 20 hours after the dose, then solved for the dose."},{"k":"algebra","t":"D0 = (4 mg/L)(18.75 L) e^((0.1195)(20)) = 75 mg × e^2.390 = 75 mg × 10.91 = 818.2 mg","why":"mg/L × L leaves mg: 75 mg is the amount in the body when the level is at the MEC. k teff has no units, and the dose must be e^2.390 = 10.91 times that 75 mg."},{"k":"round","t":"818","why":"Her key is 818 with a margin of 5%, so 777 to 859 scores. With ln 2 in place of 0.693 the dose is 818.6 mg, which rounds to 819 and also scores. Dividing by the exponential instead gives {{frac:75|10.91}} = 6.9 mg, far too small."}],"givens":[["teff","20 hours","the target duration, in the exponent"],["weight","75 kg","scales the volume: 0.25 × 75 = 18.75 L"],["t½","5.8 hours","gives k = 0.1195 hr⁻¹"],["VD","0.25 L/kg","× 75 kg = 18.75 L"],["Ceff","4 mg/L","the MEC; times VD gives 75 mg"]],"check":{"t":"20 hr is between 3 and 4 half-lives of 5.8 hr, and each half-life doubles the dose, so the dose is between 8 and 16 times the 75 mg at the MEC: 818 mg.","lo":600,"hi":1200},"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["Ceff × VD is the amount in the body at the moment the effect ends; the dose is that amount times e^(k teff).","Each added half-life of duration doubles the dose, because the target duration is in the exponent.","Scale a per-kilogram volume to the patient before using it."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 5 (Modules 7b and 8), question 4; 7b---Pharmacokinetics-and-Pharmacodynamics.pdf, slide 8 (dose for a target duration) and the In-Class Activity on slide 13"},
{id:'qpc-cq5-5', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":3,"module":8,"lecture":"qpc-L12","topic":"qpc-nonlin","sub":"qpc-kmcp","concept":"nl-low-conc","skill":"apply","dupOf":"qpc-nl-c09","source":"both","stem":"Which of the following is true for a medication that displays nonlinear pharmacokinetics?","options":[{"t":"At low concentrations of drug in the plasma relative to Vmax, the elimination rate depends on concentration.","correct":false,"why":"Picking this compares the concentration with the wrong parameter. Vmax is a rate, an amount per time, so a plasma concentration cannot be low or high relative to it; the order of elimination is read from Cp against KM, which has units of concentration."},{"t":"At low concentrations of drug in the plasma relative to KM, the elimination rate is independent of concentration.","correct":false,"why":"Picking this reverses the two ends. A rate independent of concentration is zero order, which holds when Cp is much greater than KM and the enzyme runs at Vmax. Well below KM the rate is {{frac:Vmax|KM}} × Cp, which follows the concentration."},{"t":"At low concentrations of drug in the plasma relative to KM, the elimination rate depends on the concentration.","correct":true,"why":"With Cp much less than KM, Cp is negligible in KM + Cp and the rate is {{frac:Vmax|KM}} × Cp = k′Cp. Vmax and KM are constants, so the rate is proportional to Cp: first-order behaviour."},{"t":"At low concentrations of drug in the plasma relative to Vmax, the elimination rate is independent of concentration.","correct":false,"why":"Picking this pairs the wrong parameter with the zero-order end. Vmax is a rate, not a concentration, so Cp is not compared with it; zero order applies when Cp is much greater than KM, not at low concentrations."}],"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["Rate of elimination = {{frac:Vmax × Cp|KM + Cp}}.","Cp much less than KM: the rate is {{frac:Vmax|KM}} × Cp, proportional to Cp, so first order.","Cp much greater than KM: the rate is Vmax, a constant, so zero order, and further dose increases accumulate.","KM and Cp share units of concentration, which is why they are the pair compared; Vmax is a rate."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 5 (Modules 7b and 8), question 5; 8 - Nonlinear PK.pdf, slides 7 \"Parameters\" and 8 \"Saturable-Enzymatic Elimination Processes\""},
{id:'qpc-nl-c09', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":3,"module":8,"lecture":"qpc-L12","dupOf":"qpc-nl-c01","topic":"qpc-nonlin","sub":"qpc-kmcp","concept":"nl-low-conc","skill":"apply","source":"both","stem":"Which of the following is true regarding the rate of elimination of a saturable drug when the plasma concentration (Cp) is much less than the Michaelis constant (KM)?","options":[{"t":"It equals {{frac:Vmax|KM}} × Cp, so elimination looks first order","correct":true,"why":"With Cp much less than KM, Cp is negligible in KM + Cp, leaving {{frac:Vmax|KM}} × Cp. Vmax and KM are both constants, so their ratio is a constant k′, and a rate proportional to Cp is first order."},{"t":"It equals Vmax, so elimination is zero order","why":"Choosing this applies the high-concentration limit at the wrong end. The rate equals Vmax only when Cp is much greater than KM and KM drops out."},{"t":"It does not depend on Vmax","why":"Choosing this drops Vmax along with Cp. Only Cp is negligible in the denominator; Vmax stays in the numerator and sets k′ = {{frac:Vmax|KM}}."},{"t":"It is zero, because too little drug is present to bind the enzyme","why":"Choosing this reads a low concentration as no elimination. A low concentration gives a proportionally low rate, k′Cp, which is the first-order behaviour of curve B."}],"teach":[{"h":"KM against Cp","list":["−{{frac:dCp|dt}} = {{frac:Vmax × Cp|KM + Cp}} (Michaelis-Menten kinetics).","Cp much greater than KM: KM is negligible, the rate equals Vmax and does not depend on concentration, which is zero order.","Cp much less than KM: the rate is {{frac:Vmax|KM}} × Cp = k′Cp, proportional to concentration, which is first order."]}],"cite":"8 - Nonlinear PK.pdf, slide 8 \"Saturable-Enzymatic Elimination Processes\"; transcript 10-14"},
{id:'qpc-nl-c01', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":3,"module":8,"lecture":"qpc-L12","topic":"qpc-nonlin","sub":"qpc-kmcp","concept":"nl-order-by-conc","skill":"recall","source":"transcript","stem":"Drugs that display Michaelis-Menten saturation kinetics will often exhibit zero-order kinetics at low concentrations and first-order kinetics at high concentrations.","options":[{"t":"True","why":"Choosing this reverses the two ends. Well below KM the rate is {{frac:Vmax|KM}} × Cp, proportional to concentration, which is first order; well above KM the rate equals Vmax whatever the concentration, which is zero order."},{"t":"False","correct":true,"why":"The statement has the orders the other way round. At low concentrations, Cp much less than KM, elimination looks first order; at high concentrations, Cp much greater than KM, the enzyme runs at Vmax and elimination looks zero order."}],"teach":[{"h":"KM against Cp","list":["−{{frac:dCp|dt}} = {{frac:Vmax × Cp|KM + Cp}} (Michaelis-Menten kinetics).","Cp much greater than KM: KM is negligible, the rate equals Vmax and does not depend on concentration, which is zero order.","Cp much less than KM: the rate is {{frac:Vmax|KM}} × Cp = k′Cp, proportional to concentration, which is first order."]}],"cite":"8 - Nonlinear PK.pdf, slide 8 \"Saturable-Enzymatic Elimination Processes\"; PollEv true/false, transcript 10-14 (keyed false)"},
{id:'qpc-cq5-6', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":3,"module":8,"lecture":"qpc-L12","topic":"qpc-nonlin","sub":"qpc-linear","concept":"nl-saturation-features","skill":"recall","dupOf":"qpc-nl-c04","source":"slide","stem":"Which of the following is true regarding drugs that display nonlinear pharmacokinetics?","options":[{"t":"Parameters do not change when different or multiple doses of a drug are given","correct":false,"why":"Picking this takes the definition of linear pharmacokinetics for nonlinear. With a nonlinear drug an increased dose or chronic administration changes the parameters: clearance falls and the half-life lengthens as the concentration rises."},{"t":"Half-life changes as dose is increased","correct":true,"why":"With saturable elimination t½ = VD({{frac:0.693|Vmax}})(KM + Cp), so a larger dose, giving a higher Cp, gives a longer half-life. It is one of the characteristics of saturation kinetics."},{"t":"The principle of superposition applies at steady-state.","correct":false,"why":"Picking this carries a linear rule into saturation kinetics. Superposition adds the curves of separate doses, which needs each dose to lose the same fraction per hour; once elimination saturates, a later dose is cleared more slowly, so the curves do not simply add."},{"t":"The AUC is proportional to the amount of bioavailable drug.","correct":false,"why":"Picking this describes dose-independent kinetics. With saturable elimination the AUC (area under the curve) is disproportional to the amount of bioavailable drug: past a certain dose it rises more than in proportion."}],"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["Linear: parameters such as half-life and clearance do not change when different or multiple doses are given.","Saturation kinetics: half-life changes as dose is increased, and the AUC is disproportional to the amount of bioavailable drug.","t½ = VD({{frac:0.693|Vmax}})(KM + Cp) rises with Cp; Cl = {{frac:Vmax|KM + Cp}} falls with Cp."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 5 (Modules 7b and 8), question 6; 8 - Nonlinear PK.pdf, slides 3 \"Linear vs. Nonlinear Pharmacokinetics\" and 6 \"Saturation Kinetics\""},
{id:'qpc-nl-c04', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":3,"module":8,"lecture":"qpc-L12","topic":"qpc-nonlin","sub":"qpc-linear","concept":"nl-saturation-features","skill":"recall","source":"slide","multi":true,"stem":"Which of the following are characteristics of saturation kinetics? (Select all that apply)","options":[{"t":"Elimination half-life changes as dose is increased","correct":true,"why":"With saturable elimination t½ = VD(0.693/Vmax)(KM + Cp), so a higher dose, giving a higher Cp, gives a longer half-life. In linear kinetics the half-life does not change with dose."},{"t":"AUC is disproportional to the amount of bioavailable drug","correct":true,"why":"Once elimination saturates, a larger dose is cleared more slowly, so the AUC (area under the curve) rises more than in proportion to the dose, curve A against the straight curve C."},{"t":"Other drugs using the same enzyme or carrier can affect saturation","correct":true,"why":"Two drugs that need the same enzyme or carrier compete for its limited capacity, so one can push the other toward saturation: a drug–drug interaction through a shared system."},{"t":"Metabolite ratios may change with dose","correct":true,"why":"If one metabolic route saturates at a higher dose while others do not, the share of drug going down each route changes, and so does the mix of metabolites."},{"t":"Clearance stays constant as the dose is increased, whatever the concentration","why":"Choosing this carries the linear rule into saturation kinetics. ClT = Vmax/(KM + Cp) falls as Cp rises, so clearance is usually not constant within nonlinear processes."},{"t":"AUC increases in direct proportion to the dose at every dose given","why":"Choosing this describes curve C, dose-independent kinetics, rather than saturable kinetics. With saturation the AUC versus dose line bends upward once the dose passes a certain point."}],"teach":[{"h":"Saturation kinetics","list":["Elimination kinetics is nonlinear, and the elimination half-life changes as dose is increased.","AUC is disproportional to the amount of bioavailable drug.","Saturation of capacity-limited processes may be affected by other drugs requiring the same enzyme or carrier-mediated system.","The composition or ratio of the metabolites may be affected by a change in the dose."]}],"cite":"8 - Nonlinear PK.pdf, slide 6 \"Saturation Kinetics\""},
{id:'qpc-cq5-7', type:'numeric', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":3,"module":8,"lecture":"qpc-L12","topic":"qpc-nonlin","sub":"qpc-dose","concept":"nl-dose-rate","skill":"nonlin","stem":"What daily dose rate of a non-linear drug would provide a steady-state concentration of 15 mg/L? In this patient, the KM is 7.9 mg/L and the Vmax is 625 mg/day. Provide your answer in mg/day rounded to the nearest five with no units.","units":"mg/day","answer":409,"tol":20.4,"steps":[{"k":"setup","t":"R = {{frac:Vmax × Css|KM + Css}}","why":"KM and Vmax are given, and the target Css is 15 mg/L, so the nonlinear dose-rate line answers directly. Vmax is already in mg/day, so no weight is needed."},{"k":"algebra","t":"R = {{frac:(625 mg/day)(15 mg/L)|(7.9 + 15) mg/L}} = {{frac:9375|22.9}} = 409.4 mg/day","why":"mg/L cancels top and bottom and leaves mg/day, the units of Vmax. The target Css is on top and again, added to KM, on the bottom, which keeps R below Vmax for any target."},{"k":"round","t":"R = 409 mg/day","why":"Her key is 409, the whole number; the instruction asks for the nearest five, which is 410. The margin of 5% takes 389 to 430, so both score. Leaving KM out of the bottom gives Vmax itself, 625, far too high."}],"givens":[["Css","15 mg/L","the target, top and bottom"],["KM","7.9 mg/L","added to Css in the denominator"],["Vmax","625 mg/day","the numerator; units of R"]],"check":{"t":"Css is about two-thirds of KM + Css (15 of 22.9), so R is about two-thirds of 625: near 410 mg/day, below Vmax.","lo":0,"hi":625},"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["At steady state the dose rate equals the elimination rate: R = {{frac:Vmax × Css|KM + Css}}.","R is always below Vmax; a dose rate at or above Vmax never reaches a steady state.","Give the dose rate as an amount per day, rounded as the stem asks."]}],"note":"Canvas shows 409 as the key, although the stem asks for the nearest five (410). Both are within the ±5% margin, so either scores here.","cite":"Canvas Quiz 5 (Modules 7b and 8), question 7; In-Class Practice, Nonlinear PK, question 1 (the same stem with KM 6.9 mg/L and Vmax 584 mg/day); 8 - Nonlinear PK.pdf, slide 11 \"In Vivo Determination of KM and Vmax\""},
{id:'qpc-cq5-8', type:'numeric', prof:'Mosley', "tier":"new","exam":3,"module":8,"lecture":"qpc-L12","topic":"qpc-nonlin","sub":"qpc-kmvmax","concept":"nl-km-direct","skill":"nonlin","stem":"When a 25 year-old, 75 kg patient was taking a daily oral dose of 350 mg, his steady state plasma concentration was 8.7 mg/L.When the daily dose was increased to 450 mg, a steady-state plasma concentration of 20.6 mg/L resulted. What is the KM of this drug in this patient? Provide your answer in mg/L rounded to the nearest tenth (one decimal place) with no units.","units":"mg/L","answer":5.4,"tol":0.27,"steps":[{"k":"setup","t":"KM = {{frac:R2 − R1|(R1/C1) − (R2/C2)}}, with R1 = 350 mg/day, C1 = 8.7 mg/L, R2 = 450 mg/day, C2 = 20.6 mg/L","why":"Two daily doses in the same patient and the steady-state concentration each produced: the direct method. The 75 kg weight and the age are not needed when the doses stay in mg/day."},{"k":"algebra","t":"KM = {{frac:450 − 350|(350/8.7) − (450/20.6)}} = {{frac:100|40.23 − 21.84}} = {{frac:100|18.39}} = 5.438 mg/L","why":"Each dose over its own concentration: 350 over 8.7 is 40.23 and 450 over 20.6 is 21.84. (mg/day) over (mg/day per mg/L) leaves mg/L, a concentration, the unit of KM."},{"k":"round","t":"KM = 5.4 mg/L","why":"Her key is 5.4 with a margin of 5%, so 5.13 to 5.67 scores. Pairing each dose with the other dose's concentration makes the bottom negative and gives a negative KM, which cannot be right."}],"givens":[["R1","350 mg","the first daily dose"],["C1","8.7 mg/L","steady state on 350 mg"],["R2","450 mg","the increased daily dose"],["C2","20.6 mg/L","steady state on 450 mg"],["weight","75 kg","not needed: the doses stay in mg/day"],["age","25 year-old","not needed"]],"check":{"t":"Raising the dose from 350 to 450 mg more than doubled the concentration, from 8.7 to 20.6 mg/L, the mark of saturation; KM is a concentration: 5.4 mg/L.","lo":0,"hi":20.6},"teach":[{"h":"The idea","list":["Two dose rates and their steady-state levels in one patient give KM by the direct method; Vmax follows from either pair.","Divide each dose by its own concentration; the larger dose has the smaller ratio, so the bottom is positive.","A small rise in dose giving a large rise in concentration is the sign of saturable elimination."]}],"cite":"Canvas Quiz 5 (Modules 7b and 8), question 8; In-Class Practice, Nonlinear PK, question 2 (the same stem with 9.2 and 20 mg/L); 8 - Nonlinear PK.pdf, slide 13 \"Determination of KM and Vmax by Direct Method\""},
);
